# ============================================================================
# What never enters the build context
# ============================================================================
# The build context is the whole backend folder, sent to the daemon before the
# first instruction runs. Two of these lines are not about speed:
#
#   node_modules   a tree installed on Windows carries a WINDOWS Prisma query
#                  engine and Windows-shaped paths. Copying it into a Linux
#                  image is the classic broken Node+Prisma build; `npm ci`
#                  inside the image is the whole point of the Dockerfile.
#   .env           a secret must never be in an image - and not only because of
#                  the registry copy: build contexts and layer caches outlive
#                  the file by months. Compose passes real env vars instead.
#
# Everything else is here because it is not needed to produce dist/ + the
# generated Prisma client (the build only ever reads src/, prisma/, tsconfig.json
# and the two package manifests), and a smaller context means a faster build.
# ============================================================================

node_modules
dist
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Tests are compiled by tsconfig.test.json, never by the build config (rootDir
# is ./src), so they cannot affect the artifact - and leaving them out means a
# stray test fixture can never end up in a deployed image.
tests
coverage

*.tsbuildinfo
*.log
npm-debug.log*
scratch/
*.tmp

.git
.gitignore
.vscode
README.md
tsconfig.test.json
